  "response": "The question of authorship for the oldest painted markings in Iberian caves was settled, as far as it has been settled, by chronology rather than by style. Nothing in a row of red lines or a hand stencil identifies the species that made it, so the decisive evidence had to be an age falling either within or before the window of modern human presence on the peninsula. The dating program that forced the issue had a clear leader: the Hoffmann team published its cave art dates in the journal Nature after Dirk Hoffmann led the team that dated the Iberian cave paintings. The debate that followed was therefore conducted largely over method, because any attribution could be no stronger than the ages beneath it.\n\nRadiocarbon could not be applied to mineral pigments, so the analysis took an indirect route. The team applied uranium-thorium dating to carbonate crusts that formed over the painted markings. Because the measurements were taken on deposits laid down on top of the pigment rather than within it, a carbonate crust that overlies a painting provides a minimum age for that painting: the paint must already have been on the wall when the calcite began to accumulate, and the crust says nothing about how long the image existed before it was covered. A ladder-shaped scalariform sign at La Pasiega was among the dated markings. Provided that this minimum-age logic holds, the ladder sign at La Pasiega is more than 64,000 years old; that is, the scalariform sign was painted over 64 millennia ago.\n\nDespite that antiquity, modern humans are generally thought to have arrived in Europe around 45,000 to 42,000 years ago. The age of the sign therefore poses a strict dilemma about the population of the peninsula, one with no middle ground: either modern humans were present in Iberia 64,000 years ago, or no modern humans were present in Iberia 64,000 years ago, and these two possibilities cannot both be true. Accepting the first would require an otherwise undocumented dispersal more than twenty millennia earlier than the conventional chronology allows. The Hoffmann et al. 2018 study resolved the tension by concluding that Neanderthals produced the dated Iberian cave markings. That attribution reconciles a sign older than 64,000 years with the accepted arrival window by assigning the sign to the hominin population already resident in Iberia, and it carried a substantial implication: symbolic behavior would no longer be a trait unique to Homo sapiens.\n\nBy contrast, critics argued that the uranium-thorium ages from the crusts were artificially old. That objection, as its proponents developed it, led to an open-system scenario in which at least one of two things occurred, perhaps both: the dated crust samples lost uranium after they formed, or they contained uncorrected detrital thorium. Either process pushes a uranium-thorium age upward. Leaching of uranium raises the apparent ratio of thorium to its parent isotope, and thorium carried into the calcite with clay or dust adds daughter product that did not grow in place, so each mechanism would make a young crust look ancient and its minimum age correspondingly misleading. The dating team answered by pointing to sequential sampling from the crust surface inward and to ages that increased consistently with depth, a pattern difficult to reconcile with random uranium loss. By contrast with those contested ages, the setting of the key site is not in question: La Pasiega is located in Asturias.\n\nAdjudication between Neanderthal and modern human authorship thus reduced to a narrow geochemical question about whether the crusts behaved as closed systems. If they did, the minimum ages stand and the case for Neanderthal painting follows from the arrival chronology; if they did not, the Iberian markings lose their claim to precede modern humans, and the attribution reverts to an open problem awaiting independent dating methods.",
